To defend their employees or members of the public, for instance, health and safety reasons, thwart violence and robbery of belongings, to maintain discipline in the office and other business centers.
The Laws of the United Kingdom about Surveillance include:
The implied legal requirement of trust and confidence that subsists between an employer and employee is also related – Employers should not act without logical and appropriate reason, in a way that is probable to destroy or harm the relationship of mutual trust and confidence between themselves and their employees.
Therefore, employers require finding a balance between an employee’s rightful expectation to privacy and the Employers interests when they watch their staff, in any way; there also must be a legitimate reason for the monitoring.
Targeted observation (of one individual) and systematic observation (where all employees or groups of employees are regularly observed similarly) Open and covert surveillance
Therefore when employers establish surveillance systems they must (to make certain the monitoring is legal):
Tell the staff the reason, extent, and nature of the monitoring that may occur. Staff do not go down their right to personal privacy when they walk through their Employer’s doors and this must be unbiased with the Employers right to make sure their employees are not engaging in misbehavior
Be clear what levels of privacy an employee can or cannot imagine when using their employer’s systems to make private communications, and when using restrooms or break areas that are under the vigilance
Be clear what levels of email/internet/phone usage by the employee for private reasons is permitted and what is not Give written policy accounts about the surveillance
Explain how the information will be saved and processed following the GDPR and the Data Protection Act, and who has access to this information
If this targeted surveillance provides information unintentionally of other misconduct by other workers, this proof should not be used against those members of staff unless it is a case of serious coarse misbehavior. Where the misbehavior is minor, the use of the ‘secret’ video recording to discipline a member of staff will usually not be allowed.
A close watch of employees out of the workplace may also be suitable if the employer can display it was reasonable (they have plausible causes to suggest an employee is involved in misconduct or breaching company policies) and in proportion (the employer did not go any further than was essential in its use of observation).
On May 25, 2018, when the GDPR has become law, the Information Commissioner’s Office has verified that secret monitoring of employees can only be justified in outstanding circumstances when updating the employee involved would prejudice the avoidance or detection of a crime. Employers can rely on our high-quality device for surveillance of employees, click here for more details.
Community Gateway Association versus Atkinson, in a 2014 lawsuit, the Employment Appeal Trial held that the Employer detecting an employee’s emails, in the course of a punitive investigation into the employee’s demeanor, did not amount to an unfair meddling with the employees’ personal life – the employee did not have a sensible hope of privacy in conditions where he had sent emails from his work account in breach of the e-mail policy (which he had outlined and was accountable for enforcing!)
In 2009, after witnessing irregularities between levels of stocks and sales that was worth 20,000€ over several months, the supermarket, along with visible CCTV camera, installed hidden cameras behind their cashier’s desks throughout the store. Five employees were terminated from the job later after the surveillance cameras noticed them stealing (or them assisting other employees or customers to steal).
The ECHR felt that the secret surveillance was an interruption into their personal life, as the cashiers could not shun being filmed as they were necessitated to report to work. The ECHR said that to abide by the data guard legislation the employees must openly, precisely and unmistakably be informed of the monitoring and the purpose of the surveillance.
