In this article, we will help in simplifying the concepts of YouTube copyright claims and YouTube strikes for you. Although they appear to be somewhat similar at first glance, copyright claims and strikes tend to affect channels very differently.
The YouTube Community Guidelines clearly specify that the content creators can only upload original content or content they have permission to use. If your content is not in compliance with these guidelines, it can lead to a copyright claim or a copyright strike.
They can only be applied to the flagged videos, not on the entire YouTube channel.
If you use someone else’s content, the copyright holder can claim the revenue generated from that video.
Users have the option to dispute a copyright claim if they believe it to be false.
A YouTube copyright strike is usually issued against a content creator when they upload a video containing copyrighted content without the permission of the owner. If copyright violations are found with a video, the owner has the option to take down that video from YouTube.
YouTube also imposes limitations on live streaming from your account.
If you accumulate three copyright strikes, YouTube will terminate your channel and also ban you from creating any more channels on the platform.
When copyright holders are notified about a copyright violation, they have two ways to manage their interests –
Using the Content ID tool to issue a copyright claim, which will present the holder with several options. They can monetize the video, collect ad revenue or block the video in certain regions.
Through this notification, the user can ask YouTube to legally reinstate the video that was removed because of an alleged copyright violation. In another case, the copyright owner might retract their claim if they realize they have erroneously issued a takedown notice for a video.
